{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Blue Sentinel",
          "version": "1.0.0",
          "informationUri": "https://bluesentinel.io",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "xss",
              "name": "xss",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "xss"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks occur when an attacker uses a web application to send malicious code, generally in the form of a browser side script, to a different end user. Flaws that allow these attacks to succeed are quite widespread and occur anywhere a web application uses input from a user within the output it generates without validating or encoding it. XSS attacks can be used to steal user sessions, deface websites, redirect users to malicious sites, and perform actions on behalf of users."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **Use framework auto-escape.** React's `{value}`, Vue's `{{ value }}`, lit-html's `${value}` all escape by default. Avoid `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, `v-html`, and `unsafeHTML` on user-controlled data.\n2. **For raw DOM updates use `textContent`, not `innerHTML`.** If you must produce HTML, sanitise with **DOMPurify** (`DOMPurify.sanitize(html, { USE_PROFILES: { html: true } })`) which is battle-tested and ships an `RETURN_TRUSTED_TYPE` mode.\n3. **Set Content-Security-Policy** with nonces: `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'nonce-RANDOM' 'strict-dynamic'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'`. Add `require-trusted-types-for 'script'` once ready — it converts entire classes of XSS into thrown exceptions.\n4. **Set cookies with `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite=Strict` (or Lax)** so cross-site script can't steal sessions.\n5. **Encode by output context.** `encodeURIComponent` for URL params, JSON.stringify for `<script>` blocks, HTML escape for HTML body. The OWASP Java Encoder pattern applies to Node too — pick by sink.\n6. **Run `eslint-plugin-no-unsanitized`** in CI to flag `innerHTML`, `outerHTML`, `insertAdjacentHTML`, `document.write` calls.\n\nPriority: IMMEDIATE"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-79",
                  "OWASP/A03:2021"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "cwe": "CWE-79",
                "owasp": "A03:2021 - Injection"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "prototype_pollution",
              "name": "prototype_pollution",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "prototype_pollution"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When user-supplied data is merged into an object without rejecting the special keys __proto__, constructor, and prototype, an attacker can write properties onto Object.prototype. Because almost every object inherits from it, the injected property appears globally — including on objects that are later used for authorization decisions, configuration, or template rendering. The impact ranges from privilege escalation (injecting isAdmin: true so a later check passes) through denial of service to remote code execution, where a polluted property reaches a template engine or child-process option and is interpreted as code. The bug is often introduced by a small utility function or an outdated version of a popular library such as lodash."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **Block the dangerous keys** explicitly in any recursive merge or assignment path.\n2. **Use safe containers.** Prefer `Map`, or `Object.create(null)`, for data derived from user input so there is no prototype to pollute.\n3. **Freeze the prototype.** `Object.freeze(Object.prototype)` at startup blocks the write globally where compatible.\n4. **Update dependencies.** Upgrade lodash (≥4.17.21) and any other library with known pollution advisories.\n5. **Validate with a schema.** Parse request bodies into typed objects (JSON Schema, zod, joi) instead of merging raw input.\n6. **Do not rely on inherited properties** for security decisions; use `Object.hasOwn()` when reading flags.\n\nPriority: IMMEDIATE"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-1321"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "cwe": "CWE-1321"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "request",
              "name": "request",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "request"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Security vulnerability of type request detected."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Review the code and implement proper security controls based on the vulnerability type."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-0"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5",
                "cwe": "CWE-0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "code_injection",
              "name": "code_injection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "code_injection"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Code injection is a general term for attack types that involve injecting code that is then interpreted and executed by the application. This type of attack exploits poor handling of untrusted data. These types of attacks are usually made possible due to a lack of proper input/output data validation, for example when a user input is directly concatenated into a dynamically evaluated code snippet."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **Eradicate `eval()`, `new Function(...)`, `setTimeout(string, ...)`, `setInterval(string, ...)`** from any path that touches user input. There is no safe way to evaluate untrusted JavaScript source — even `vm.runInNewContext` and `vm.runInNewVM` have escapes.\n2. **Replace 'parse a config blob' use cases with `JSON.parse()` or `YAML.parse()` (yaml package, safe schema).** If the blob must support expressions, use a tiny purpose-built DSL (e.g. JEXL with safe context) — never the full JS engine.\n3. **For browser-side dynamic templates**, use a templating library with auto-escape (lit-html, Vue, React JSX) instead of `innerHTML = string` or `document.write`.\n4. **Never use `child_process.exec(string)` from user input** — that's command injection. Use `execFile(file, [args])` with the array form.\n5. **Set a Content-Security-Policy** that forbids inline scripts and `eval`: `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; require-trusted-types-for 'script'`. CSP catches eval at runtime.\n6. **Run `eslint-plugin-security` and `eslint-plugin-no-unsanitized`** in CI — they flag `eval`, `Function`, `setTimeout/setInterval` with non-literal first arguments.\n\nPriority: IMMEDIATE"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-94",
                  "OWASP/A03:2021"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "cwe": "CWE-94",
                "owasp": "A03:2021 - Injection"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "open_redirect",
              "name": "open_redirect",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "open_redirect"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Open redirects borrow the credibility of your domain. A link that begins with your hostname passes casual inspection, survives many email filters, and is trusted by users — yet lands on an attacker-controlled page. Beyond phishing, redirects are a standard component of OAuth and SSO flows, so an unvalidated redirect_uri can leak authorization codes or tokens to the attacker, converting a 'low severity' finding into full account takeover. They are also used to bypass SSRF and CSRF defences that allow-list the trusted host."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **Map, don't pass.** Accept an identifier (`?next=dashboard`) and resolve it server-side to a fixed URL.\n2. **Allow-list hosts** if external redirects are genuinely required, comparing the parsed host exactly — not with `startswith`.\n3. **Accept relative paths only**, rejecting values containing a scheme, `//`, or a backslash.\n4. **Pin OAuth redirect_uri** to pre-registered exact values; never allow wildcards.\n5. **Warn on exit** with an interstitial for any permitted external redirect.\n6. **Test** with protocol-relative and encoded payloads to confirm the check holds.\n\nPriority: HIGH"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-601",
                  "OWASP/A01:2021"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5",
                "cwe": "CWE-601",
                "owasp": "A01:2021 - Broken Access Control"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "insecure_cookie",
              "name": "insecure_cookie",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "insecure_cookie"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Each missing attribute removes a distinct protection. Without HttpOnly the cookie is readable by JavaScript, so any XSS becomes full session theft. Without Secure it is transmitted over plain HTTP, where a network attacker can capture it. Without SameSite the browser attaches it to cross-site requests, enabling CSRF. These flags cost nothing and are the cheapest hardening available for session management, yet defaults in many frameworks still omit them."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **HttpOnly** on anything the client script never needs to read.\n2. **Secure** always, and serve the site over HTTPS with HSTS.\n3. **SameSite=Lax** by default (Strict for admin flows); use None only with Secure and a documented reason.\n4. **Scope tightly** — narrow Path/Domain rather than the registrable domain.\n5. **Rotate on privilege change** and invalidate server-side on logout.\n6. **Set it centrally** in framework session config so individual handlers cannot regress it.\n\nPriority: HIGH"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-1004",
                  "OWASP/A05:2021"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5",
                "cwe": "CWE-1004",
                "owasp": "A05:2021 - Security Misconfiguration"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "inefficient_regex_redos",
              "name": "inefficient_regex_redos",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "inefficient_regex_redos"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Backtracking regex engines explore every way a pattern can match. Patterns containing nested or adjacent quantifiers over overlapping character classes — (a+)+, (a|a)*, (\\\\s*)*$ — have exponentially many candidate paths on a non-matching input, so a request of a few dozen characters can pin a CPU core for seconds or minutes. Because most web servers evaluate regexes on the request thread, a handful of such requests exhausts the worker pool and takes the service down without any authentication."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **Remove ambiguity.** Avoid nested quantifiers over overlapping classes ((a+)+, (a|a)*); make alternatives mutually exclusive and anchor patterns.\n2. **Bound the input.** Reject over-length values BEFORE evaluating the regex — this alone defeats most ReDoS.\n3. **Use a linear-time engine** (RE2, rust regex) or a library-level timeout where available.\n4. **Prefer parsers over regex** for structured formats such as URLs and emails.\n5. **Scan patterns in CI** with a ReDoS linter and keep regex-carrying dependencies current.\n\nPriority: HIGH"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-1333"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5",
                "cwe": "CWE-1333"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "path_traversal",
              "name": "path_traversal",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "path_traversal"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Path traversal attacks (also known as directory traversal) aim to access files and directories that are stored outside the web root folder. In C# applications, this typically occurs through File.ReadAllText, FileStream, StreamReader, or similar file operations. By manipulating variables that reference files with \"dot-dot-slash (../)\" sequences and its variations or by using absolute file paths, it may be possible to access arbitrary files and directories stored on file system."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **Confine paths**: `var root = Path.GetFullPath(\"/var/app/uploads\"); var target = Path.GetFullPath(Path.Combine(root, userPath)); if (!target.StartsWith(root + Path.DirectorySeparatorChar)) throw new UnauthorizedAccessException();`. `Path.GetFullPath` resolves `..` segments before the comparison.\n2. **Reject control characters and reserved names.** Block input containing `\\0`, `\\r`, `\\n`, characters in `Path.GetInvalidFileNameChars()`, and Windows reserved names (`CON`, `PRN`, `AUX`, `NUL`, `COM1`–`COM9`, `LPT1`–`LPT9`).\n3. **Use indirect identifiers.** Map opaque user IDs to filenames server-side: `var file = REPORTS.TryGetValue(id, out var f) ? f : throw new FileNotFoundException();`.\n4. **Use `IFileProvider`** (e.g. `PhysicalFileProvider` rooted at the safe directory) for static-file delivery. ASP.NET Core checks the boundary internally.\n5. **Keep file uploads out of the web-root.** Store under a non-served directory and serve via a download action that authorises the request and streams the file.\n6. **Run `SecurityCodeScan` rule `SCS0018`** (path traversal) — it flags `Path.Combine` followed by direct file ops on user input.\n\nPriority: IMMEDIATE"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-22",
                  "OWASP/A01:2021"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5",
                "cwe": "CWE-22",
                "owasp": "A01:2021 - Broken Access Control"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "reflection",
              "name": "reflection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "reflection"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Security vulnerability of type reflection detected."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Review the code and implement proper security controls based on the vulnerability type."
