            {
              "id": "deserialization",
              "name": "deserialization",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "deserialization"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Java deserialization vulnerabilities arise when applications deserialize untrusted data using ObjectInputStream.readObject() or similar methods. Since Java serialization can reconstruct entire object graphs including executable code, attackers can craft malicious serialized objects that execute arbitrary code during deserialization. This is particularly dangerous because it can lead to remote code execution without any user interaction."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **Replace Java serialization with Jackson + JSON**. `ObjectInputStream.readObject()` on untrusted bytes is RCE; the only safe path is to abandon Java serialization for any external boundary.\n2. **Disable Jackson polymorphic / default typing.** `objectMapper.deactivateDefaultTyping();`. Use explicit `@JsonTypeInfo` with `Id.NAME` and a registered subtype list — never `Id.CLASS` or `Id.MINIMAL_CLASS`.\n3. **If Java serialization is unavoidable, install a Look-Ahead Object Input Stream** (e.g. Apache Commons IO `ValidatingObjectInputStream`) with a strict allowlist of accepted classes. Reject the rest.\n4. **Drop XStream from new code** unless you can keep the allowlist + security framework current. Same for Snake YAML — use the SafeConstructor.\n5. **Patch known gadget chains.** Run `dependency-check` / Snyk / OSV scanner against the dependency tree; Commons Collections, Spring Core, Hibernate, Apache Log4j 1.x, and Apache CXF have all shipped gadget chains.\n6. **Avoid `RMI` / `JMX` / `JNDI lookup` on remote URIs.** These transport Java-serialized objects by default and have produced decades of RCE bugs (Log4Shell being the most famous).\n\nPriority: IMMEDIATE"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-502",
                  "OWASP/A08:2021"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5",
                "cwe": "CWE-502",
                "owasp": "A08:2021 - Software and Data Integrity Failures"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "sql_injection",
              "name": "sql_injection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "sql_injection"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "SQL injection is a code injection technique that exploits security vulnerabilities in the database layer of Java applications. The vulnerability occurs when user input is incorrectly filtered for string literal escape characters embedded in SQL statements or user input is not strongly typed. SQL injection attacks can bypass application security measures, authentication and authorization, and retrieve the contents of entire databases."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **Use `PreparedStatement` with bound parameters**: `PreparedStatement p = c.prepareStatement(\"SELECT * FROM u WHERE id = ?\"); p.setLong(1, id); ResultSet rs = p.executeQuery();`. Never use `Statement` for queries that include user input.\n2. **For JPA/Hibernate use `setParameter`**: `em.createQuery(\"SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.email = :e\").setParameter(\"e\", email)`. Avoid `createNativeQuery` with concatenated strings, and avoid `@Query` annotations that interpolate `#{...}`.\n3. **For Spring Data JPA prefer derived methods or @Query with named params.** Spring Data's `findByEmail(email)` is parameterised; `@Query(\"SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.email = ?1\")` with positional binding is also safe.\n4. **Allowlist identifiers.** Column / table names cannot be parameterised — validate them against a fixed list: `String col = ALLOWED_SORT.contains(input) ? input : \"name\";`.\n5. **Apply DB-level least privilege.** The web user gets `SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE` only — no `DROP`, `ALTER`, or DBA. Use a separate DB account per microservice.\n6. **Run static analysis in CI**: SpotBugs + FindSecBugs catches `SQL_INJECTION_JDBC`, `SQL_INJECTION_HIBERNATE`, `SQL_INJECTION_JPA` patterns. Make those rules build-failures.\n\nPriority: IMMEDIATE"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-89",
                  "OWASP/A03:2021"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "cwe": "CWE-89",
                "owasp": "A03:2021 - Injection"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "missing_auth_check",
              "name": "missing_auth_check",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "missing_auth_check"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Security vulnerability of type missing_auth_check detected."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Review the code and implement proper security controls based on the vulnerability type."
              },
              "properties": {
