            {
              "id": "missing_authorization",
              "name": "missing_authorization",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "missing_authorization"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Rather than an endpoint that was never protected, this is a route *around* an existing control: an early return, an exception handler, a cached branch, a debug or feature-flag shortcut, or a conditional that skips the guard for certain inputs. These are dangerous precisely because the control appears present during review — the code reads as protected, and only path analysis reveals the gap. Common concrete forms include checks placed after the sensitive action, guards that only run when an optional parameter is present, comparisons that treat a missing token as a match, and administrative shortcuts left enabled outside development."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **Check before acting.** Perform authorization before any side effect, never after.\n2. **Enforce centrally.** Use middleware, a filter, or a policy layer that runs for every request, instead of per-branch checks that can be bypassed.\n3. **Fail closed.** Every exception path, timeout, and unexpected value must deny access — never default to allow.\n4. **Delete shortcuts.** Remove debug/impersonation bypasses from production builds; gate them behind a build flag that cannot ship.\n5. **Use constant-time, type-safe comparison** for tokens, rejecting null/empty explicitly.\n6. **Test negatively.** Add tests for each identified bypass path asserting a 401/403.\n\nPriority: IMMEDIATE"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-862",
                  "OWASP/A01:2021"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5",
                "cwe": "CWE-862",
                "owasp": "A01:2021 - Broken Access Control"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "xxe",
              "name": "xxe",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "xxe"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Java applications that parse XML data are vulnerable to XXE attacks when XML parsers are configured to process external entities. Attackers can craft malicious XML documents that reference external entities, leading to disclosure of internal files, denial of service through resource exhaustion, or server-side request forgery attacks."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **Harden `DocumentBuilderFactory`**: ```java\nDocumentBuilderFactory dbf = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();\ndbf.setFeature(\"http://apache.org/xml/features/disallow-doctype-decl\", true);\ndbf.setFeature(\"http://xml.org/sax/features/external-general-entities\", false);\ndbf.setFeature(\"http://xml.org/sax/features/external-parameter-entities\", false);\ndbf.setExpandEntityReferences(false);\n``` Apply the same to `SAXParserFactory`, `XMLInputFactory`, `TransformerFactory`, `Validator`.\n2. **For `XMLInputFactory` (StAX)**: `xif.setProperty(XMLInputFactory.SUPPORT_DTD, false); xif.setProperty(\"javax.xml.stream.isSupportingExternalEntities\", false);`.\n3. **For `TransformerFactory`**: `tf.setAttribute(XMLConstants.ACCESS_EXTERNAL_DTD, \"\"); tf.setAttribute(XMLConstants.ACCESS_EXTERNAL_STYLESHEET, \"\");`.\n4. **For `Schema` validation**: same pattern with `ACCESS_EXTERNAL_SCHEMA`.\n5. **Centralise XML parsing in a hardened utility class** so every parse goes through the same secured factory. Audit the codebase for direct `DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance()` calls and route them through the utility.\n6. **Audit downstream tooling.** Office documents (DOCX/XLSX/ODT), SOAP, SAML, SVG, RSS, sitemap.xml all parse XML. FindSecBugs detects `XXE_DOCUMENT`, `XXE_SAXPARSER`, `XXE_XMLREADER`, `XXE_XMLSTREAMREADER`, `XXE_DTD_TRANSFORM_FACTORY`, etc.\n\nPriority: IMMEDIATE"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-611",
                  "OWASP/A05:2021"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "cwe": "CWE-611",
                "owasp": "A05:2021 - Security Misconfiguration"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "hardcoded_secret",
              "name": "hardcoded_secret",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "hardcoded_secret"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Embedded secrets are disclosed to everyone who can read the code — every developer, every CI job, every fork, and anyone who obtains the built artifact or container image. Version control makes this worse: a secret committed once remains in history even after it is deleted from the current file, so the exposure window is permanent until the credential is rotated at its source. Public repositories are continuously scraped by automated scanners that harvest keys within minutes of a push. Because the value is fixed at build time, incident response is slow: rotating requires editing code, re-reviewing, rebuilding, and redeploying, during which the compromised credential usually remains valid."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **Rotate first.** Assume disclosure: revoke and reissue the credential before anything else. Cleaning the code without rotating leaves the attacker's copy valid.\n2. **Externalise.** Read the value at runtime from an environment variable or a managed store (AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault, HashiCorp Vault) — prefer short-lived, automatically rotated credentials.\n3. **Purge history if warranted.** Deleting the line does not remove it from git history; use `git filter-repo` or BFG when the repository is shared or public.\n4. **Prevent recurrence.** Add a pre-commit secret scanner and a CI gate that fails the build on detection.\n5. **Prefer keyless auth.** Where the platform supports it, use workload identity / OIDC federation so no long-lived secret exists to leak.\n\nPriority: IMMEDIATE"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-798",
                  "OWASP/A07:2021"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "cwe": "CWE-798",
                "owasp": "A07:2021 - Identification and Authentication Failures"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ldap_injection",
              "name": "Ldap Injection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Ldap Injection"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "LDAP injection is a type of injection attack that exploits web-based applications that construct LDAP statements based on user input. In C# applications, this typically involves DirectorySearcher, DirectoryEntry, or similar Active Directory operations. When an application fails to properly sanitize user input before constructing and executing LDAP queries, it creates an opportunity for attackers to inject malicious LDAP statements."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **Escape every interpolated value into LDAP filters** per RFC 4515: replace `\\` `*` `(` `)` `\\0` with `\\5c`, `\\2a`, `\\28`, `\\29`, `\\00`. `Novell.Directory.Ldap` and `System.DirectoryServices.Protocols` do NOT escape for you.\n2. **Use a helper**: `static string EscapeLdapFilter(string s) => string.Concat(s.Select(c => c switch { '\\\\' => \"\\\\5c\", '*' => \"\\\\2a\", '(' => \"\\\\28\", ')' => \"\\\\29\", '\\0' => \"\\\\00\", _ => c.ToString() }));`. Apply to every user-supplied piece.\n3. **For DN composition use the DN escaping rules (RFC 4514)** which differ from filter rules: escape `, + \" \\ < > ;` plus leading/trailing space.\n4. **Bind under a read-only service account** for authentication queries. The bind account should not be able to write, delete, or enumerate sensitive attributes (e.g. `userPassword` hashes).\n5. **Use LDAPS or StartTLS with certificate verification.** Cleartext LDAP exposes credentials. Pin the directory CA via `LdapConnection.SessionOptions.VerifyServerCertificate`.\n6. **Apply rate limiting + lockout** on login endpoints — boolean blind LDAP injection can enumerate users at scale.\n\nPriority: IMMEDIATE"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-90"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "9",
                "cwe": "CWE-90"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "tainted_format_string",
