            {
              "id": "network_issues",
              "name": "network_issues",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "network_issues"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Cleartext transport exposes traffic to anyone on the path: other users of the same Wi-Fi, a compromised router, a hostile ISP, or an attacker performing ARP/DNS spoofing. Beyond passive disclosure, the absence of TLS means the endpoint is not authenticated, so an active attacker can modify responses — injecting scripts into returned HTML, tampering with downloaded updates or dependencies, or redirecting the client entirely. This is especially severe for API calls carrying bearer tokens or session cookies, and for any URL used to fetch executable content or configuration."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **Switch to https://** and verify the endpoint presents a valid certificate — never disable verification to make it work.\n2. **Enforce transport policy.** Redirect all HTTP to HTTPS and send `Strict-Transport-Security` so browsers refuse the cleartext version.\n3. **Block cleartext at the platform level** (Android `cleartextTrafficPermitted=false`, iOS ATS, .NET `RequireHttps`).\n4. **Pin or restrict where warranted** for high-value machine-to-machine calls.\n5. **Exempt deliberately.** Genuine local-only URLs (http://localhost in tests) should be annotated so the finding is not re-raised.\n\nPriority: HIGH"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-918",
                  "OWASP/A10:2021"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "cwe": "CWE-918",
                "owasp": "A10:2021 - Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)"
              }
            },
