            {
              "id": "http_response_splitting",
              "name": "HTTP Response Splitting",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "HTTP Response Splitting"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "HTTP Response Splitting attacks occur when an application includes user-controlled data in HTTP response headers without proper validation. In Go web applications, this typically happens when using http.ResponseWriter.Header().Set() or similar functions with untrusted input. Attackers can inject carriage return (\\r) and line feed (\\n) characters to split the response and inject additional headers or even a second response, potentially leading to cache poisoning, cross-site scripting, or other attacks."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **Go's `net/http` package rejects CR/LF in header values** since Go 1.6 (issue 7202). On modern Go (1.18+), `Header().Set(k, v)` with embedded `\\r\\n` returns an `http.ErrAbortHandler`-like state. Verify your runtime is current.\n2. **Validate header values explicitly** for defence-in-depth: `if strings.ContainsAny(v, \"\\r\\n\") { return errors.New(\"invalid header\") }`.\n3. **Validate redirect targets.** `http.Redirect(w, r, userURL, ...)` lets the user-supplied URL appear in the `Location` header. Allowlist target paths or hosts: `if !strings.HasPrefix(userURL, \"/\") { reject }`.\n4. **Avoid unfiltered cookie values.** `http.Cookie.Value` is escaped, but other libraries may not escape — verify or sanitise.\n5. **Use a structured logger** (`log/slog` in Go 1.21+) for log writes — never `log.Printf(\"%s\", userInput)` to log files that are shipped to a terminal; CR/LF in user data corrupts log parsing.\n6. **Run `gosec G201/G202` and `staticcheck`** to flag user input flowing into `Header().Set` without validation.\n\nPriority: HIGH"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-113",
                  "OWASP/A03:2021"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5",
                "cwe": "CWE-113",
                "owasp": "A03:2021 - Injection"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "information_exposure_via_error_error_message_exposure",
              "name": "Information Exposure via Error (error message exposure)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Information Exposure via Error (error message exposure)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Verbose errors are a reconnaissance gift. Stack traces reveal absolute paths, framework and library versions, and internal class or module names; database errors expose table and column names and often confirm injection points; and configuration dumps can leak credentials outright. None of this is exploitable on its own, which is why it is frequently dismissed — but it materially shortens the time an attacker needs to find and weaponise a real flaw, and it turns blind injection into a straightforward one by providing direct feedback."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **Separate audiences.** Show users a neutral message plus a reference ID; send the stack trace to your logging pipeline only.\n2. **Handle globally.** Register a catch-all error handler so no unhandled exception can reach the client.\n3. **Disable debug output** in production configuration (see also the debug_mode finding).\n4. **Sanitise messages** from lower layers — never pass a raw database or filesystem error through to the response.\n5. **Set custom error pages** for 4xx/5xx at the web-server level as a backstop.\n6. **Test** that forced errors return no internal detail.\n\nPriority: MEDIUM"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-209",
                  "OWASP/A04:2021"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5",
                "cwe": "CWE-209",
                "owasp": "A04:2021 - Insecure Design"
              }
            },
