            {
              "id": "insecure_randomness",
              "name": "Insecure Randomness",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Insecure Randomness"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "General-purpose PRNGs (Python's random, Java's java.util.Random, JavaScript's Math.random, C's rand) are built for statistical quality and speed, not unpredictability. They are deterministic functions of an internal state that an attacker can often recover after observing a modest number of outputs — the Mersenne Twister used by Python and Java requires 624 observed 32-bit outputs to reconstruct state and predict all future values. Seeding from the clock makes it worse, reducing the search space to the seconds around a known event. Any token generated this way — password reset links, session IDs, API keys, CSRF tokens, OTPs — is predictable rather than secret."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. **Switch the API.** Python: `secrets.token_urlsafe(32)`; Java: `java.security.SecureRandom`; Node: `crypto.randomBytes(32)`; .NET: `RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes`; Go: `crypto/rand`.\n2. **Size it properly.** At least 128 bits (16 bytes) of entropy for tokens and session identifiers.\n3. **Never seed manually** with time, PID, or another predictable value; let the OS CSPRNG seed itself.\n4. **Keep fast PRNGs for non-security use** only (simulation, sampling, test data) and comment the distinction.\n5. **Bind and expire.** Tie tokens to a user and a short lifetime so prediction has a narrow window.\n\nPriority: HIGH"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-330",
                  "OWASP/A02:2021"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "cwe": "CWE-330",
                "owasp": "A02:2021 - Cryptographic Failures"
              }
            },
